\section{\code{category} describes; it does not decide}{


\code{addr_category()} returns raddr's one-word vocabulary, and a policy layer
must not enumerate it. Label vocabularies drift -- \code{ipaddr.js} renamed
\code{deprecated} to \code{deprecatedOrchid} -- and a consumer that denies by named
list turns every newly added level into a bypass. Policy belongs on the five
IANA columns, the classify codes and the embeddings, all of which are
three-valued and registry- or RFC-sourced (P8). See \code{\link[=addr_category_map]{addr_category_map()}}.
}

\section{Embeddings are plural, and Teredo is why}{


\code{addr_embeddings()} always returns the typed list, never a single address.
RFC 4380 section 4 puts two IPv4 addresses in a Teredo address -- a server in
the clear and a bitwise-complemented client -- and section 5.2.6 makes the
server a destination a host actually sends to, so neither is metadata for the
other. There is deliberately no scalar accessor: reducing the pair to one
address \emph{is} the Teredo decision, and it is the consumer's to make.

Each row carries the \code{category} of the \strong{extracted} address, not of the one
it came out of. \code{addr_classify(addr_pton("::ffff:127.0.0.1"))} therefore
reports an IPv6 address in \verb{::ffff:0:0/96} \emph{and} an embedded \verb{127.0.0.1}
that is \code{loopback}, with neither fact collapsed into the other.
}

